  "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e8(\\operatorname{ln}\u20613)\\,{3}^{t},\\,-2(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612)\\,{2}^{t}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int (\\operatorname{ln}\u20613)\\,{3}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int -2(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612)\\,{2}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{3}^{t}+{C}_{1},-{2}^{t+1}+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=\u27e82,-1\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =\u27e8{3}^{0}+{C}_{1},-{2}^{0+1}+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e82,-1\u27e9 & =\u27e81+{C}_{1},-2+{C}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =-2+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=\u27e8{3}^{t}+1,\\,-{2}^{t+1}+1\u27e9.\n$$",
